From 333e44d3c8d15e4baf3f9ac49d576ff7bd29ca19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Argo <argo@argobox.com>
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2026 23:57:25 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] fix(desktop): scope profile endpoints to an isolated
 dashboard's profile
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Root cause: every /api/profiles/{name}/* endpoint resolved and operated on
ANY named profile with no scope check. A dashboard launched with `--isolated`
(which is documented to run a server "scoped to that profile") could
therefore read, rewrite, export, or even delete a DIFFERENT profile's data —
SOUL.md persona injection (#91330), and equally model/description mutation,
config export (config/.env exfiltration), and profile deletion.

Impact: an attacker able to reach an isolated dashboard can inject a
malicious persona into another profile, exfiltrate its config/secrets via
export, or destroy it.

Fix: track isolation explicitly. `--isolated` is threaded from the launch
flag through start_server() into app.state.isolated. All {name}-scoped
profiles endpoints (soul GET/PUT, description, model, describe-auto, export,
delete, rename, open-terminal, setup-command, desktop-overlay) route through
_assert_profile_in_scope(), which returns 403 when the server is isolated and
the requested profile is not the one it is scoped to. Isolation is signalled
by the flag itself, not inferred from the profile name, so an isolated server
scoped to the default profile is protected too. The unified machine dashboard
(isolated=False) keeps its intentional machine-wide cross-profile management
behavior; an isolated server's own profile still works.

Regression: 7 new tests cover cross-profile soul read/write refusal for a
named isolated profile, the default-profile isolated case, cross-profile
delete/export refusal, same-profile success, the machine-dashboard controls,
and isolated-state teardown.

Deferred (codex P2, written rationale): local desktop per-profile backends
are launched `--profile X serve` without `--isolated` (apps/desktop/electron/
backend-command.ts serveBackendArgs), so they retain cross-profile access.
Marking those launches `--isolated` is the intended follow-up but
deliberately NOT done here: the desktop profile switcher routes SOUL/settings
edits for a non-active profile through the active per-profile backend, and
that cross-profile UX must be validated before this PR enforces the boundary
on it — this PR scopes the fix to the reported `--isolated` dashboard vector
and leaves the desktop-launcher extension to maintainers who can verify the
desktop routing behavior.

Closes #91330
